example.paragraph:
Given $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, find $4\mathbf{u}$, $( - 3)\mathbf{v}$, and $4\mathbf{u} + ( - 3)\mathbf{v}$.

content.paragraph:
Sometimes, for convenience (and also to save space), this text may write a column vector such as $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ in the form $\left( 3\text{,~−}1 \right)$. In this case, the parentheses and the comma distinguish the vector $\left( 3\text{,~−}1 \right)$ from the $1 \times 2$ row matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      3 & {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, written with brackets and no comma. Thus

The geometric visualization of a vector such as $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is often aided by including an arrow (directed line segment) from the origin $(0\text{,~}0)$ to the point $\left( 3\text{,~−}1 \right)$, as in Figure 2. In this case, the individual points along the arrow itself have no special significance.2

example.paragraph:
The vectors $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 6} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 4} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ are displayed in Figure 4.

Let $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Display the vectors $\mathbf{u}$, $2\mathbf{u}$, and $- \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{u}$ on a graph.

See Figure 5, where $\mathbf{u}$, $2\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      6 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $- \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix}
      {- 2} \\
      {2\text{/}3}
      \end{bmatrix}$ are displayed. The arrow for $2\mathbf{u}$ is twice as long as the arrow for $\mathbf{u}$, and the arrows point in the same direction. The arrow for $- \frac{2}{3}\mathbf{u}$ is two-thirds the length of the arrow for $\mathbf{u}$, and the arrows point in opposite directions. In general, the length of the arrow for $c\mathbf{u}$ is $|c|$ times the length of the arrow for $\mathbf{u}$. [Recall that the length of the line segment from $(0\text{,~}0)$ to $(a\text{,~}b)$ is $\sqrt{a^{2} + b^{2}}$].

Let $\mathbf{a}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 2} \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ , $\mathbf{a}_{2} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      5 \\
      6
      \end{bmatrix}$ , and $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      4 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ . Determine whether $\mathbf{b}$ can be generated (or written) as a linear combination of $\mathbf{a}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{a}_{2}$. That is, determine whether weights $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ exist such that

content.paragraph:
has a solution, or, equivalently, asking whether the linear system with augmented matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{llll}
      \textbf{v}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{v}_{p} & \textbf{b}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ has a solution.

example.paragraph:
Let $\mathbf{a}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 2} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{a}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      {- 13} \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ and $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      8 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ Then $\text{Span}\{\mathbf{a}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{a}_{2}\}$ is a plane through the origin in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$. Is $\mathbf{b}$ in that plane?

Does the equation $x_{1}\mathbf{a}_{1} + x_{2}\mathbf{a}_{2} = \mathbf{b}$ have a solution? To answer this, row reduce the augmented matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \textbf{a}_{2} & \textbf{b}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$:

statement.paragraph:
Let $\mathbf{a}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      4 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{a}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      {- 3} \\
      7
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ and $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      1 \\
      h
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ For what value(s) of $h$ is $\mathbf{b}$ in the plane spanned by $\mathbf{a}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{a}_{2}\text{?}$

Let $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      1 \\
      8
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ and $\mathbf{y} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      h \\
      {- 5} \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ For what value(s) of $h$ is $\mathbf{y}$ in the plane generated by $\mathbf{v}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{v}_{2}\text{?}$

Give a geometric description of $\text{Span\,\,\{}\mathbf{v}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}_{2}\text{\}}$ for the vectors $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      8 \\
      2 \\
      {- 6}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      12 \\
      3 \\
      {- 9}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$

Let $\mathbf{u} = \,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{v} = \,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Show that $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      h \\
      k
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is in $\text{Span\,\,\{}\mathbf{u}\text{,~}\mathbf{v}\text{\}}$ for all $h$ and $k$.

(T/F) The solution set of the linear system whose augmented matrix is $\lbrack\begin{array}{rrrr}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \mathbf{a}_{2} & \mathbf{a}_{3} & \mathbf{b}
      \end{array}\rbrack$ is the same as the solution set of the equation $x_{1}\mathbf{a}_{1} + x_{2}\mathbf{a}_{2} + x_{3}\mathbf{a}_{3} = \mathbf{b}$.

(T/F) Asking whether the linear system corresponding to an augmented matrix $\lbrack\begin{array}{rrrr}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \mathbf{a}_{2} & \mathbf{a}_{3} & \mathbf{b}
      \end{array}\rbrack$ has a solution amounts to asking whether $\mathbf{b}$ is in $\text{Span\,\,\,\{}\mathbf{a}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{a}_{2}\text{,~}\mathbf{a}_{3}\text{\}}$.

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      1 & 0 & {- 4} \\
      0 & 3 & {- 2} \\
      {- 2} & 6 & 3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      1 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Denote the columns of $A$ by $\mathbf{a}_{1}$, $\mathbf{a}_{2}$, $\mathbf{a}_{3}$, and let $W = \text{Span\,\,\{}\mathbf{a}_{1}\text{,~}\mathbf{a}_{2}\text{,~}\mathbf{a}_{3}\text{\}}$.
